{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Sell me your birthright this day.</b> The prerogative of the firstborn in that time was not to receive a double portion in his father's property as we were afterward commanded in the Torah, but rather the prerogative of authority and honor after the father — that he would be in the father's place to exercise authority over the rest of his brothers, and they would honor him as their father; and also in property he would have advantage and superiority over them. And this is what Esau meant by 'what is this birthright to me' — for my trade, which is hunting, is likely to cause me to die by it during my father's lifetime, when I go to hunt the beasts in the fields and wildernesses; if so, what benefit do I derive from the birthright? And this is the meaning of what is said, ״ויאכל וישת ויקם וילך״ — to show that after he ate and drank he returned to his trade and went to the field for his hunting; and this was his way and practice all his days, to eat and drink and hunt and pursue the lusts — and this was the cause of his contempt for the birthright."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:11.418828906Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}